WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:20.940
Copernicus: A Revolution of Astronomical Proportions
It is very rare that a book can come out and

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:24.350
single-handedly change humanity’s understanding
of the world.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:39.360
But 
in 1543, De revolutionibus orbium coelestium

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.670
(“On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres”)
did just that.

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:49.140
It was a book by Polish astronomer Nicolaus
Copernicus in which he presented his model

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.870
of a heliocentric Universe - one which did
not place the Earth at the center of it all,

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:54.870
anymore.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:58.820
Instead, it had been replaced with the Sun
which was now, more or less, at the heart

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:03.390
of the Universe, and all the stars and planets
in the sky revolved around it.

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:08.360
It was a revolutionary thought, one that challenged
well-established notions that had been around

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:09.360
for over a millennium.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.820
It wasn’t immediately accepted, or even
understood, for that matter.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:18.440
But it served its purpose in presenting an
alternative to a grand idea that seemed immovable

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.150
from the public consciousness.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:24.350
Many others followed who popularized, improved
and heavily modified the heliocentric model

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:30.030
first outlined by Copernicus, but it still
remains to this day one of the core concepts

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:33.010
of our reality.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:40.800
Early Life
Nicolaus Copernicus, or Mikołaj Kopernik

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:47.590
in its original Polish, was born on February
19, 1473, in the city of Toruń in the recently

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:49.620
established territory of Royal Prussia.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:54.320
Prior to this, the region had been part of
the State of the Teutonic Order, formed by

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.060
crusader knights during the 13th century.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:02.320
However, following the Second Peace of Toruń
in 1466, Royal Prussia became an autonomous

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.200
dependency of the Kingdom of Poland.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.479
Copernicus was the son of Nicolaus the Elder
and Barbara Watzenrode.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:14.140
According to records, Nicolaus’s father
was a successful merchant, as was his grandfather

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.860
on his mother’s side.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:20.170
Originally established in Krakow, Nicolaus
the Elder decided to relocate the family to

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.280
Toruń before the birth of his namesake.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:27.120
Nicolaus was the youngest in the family with
an older brother and two older sisters.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:31.890
The family last name comes from Koperniki,
the village where his paternal ancestors came

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:32.890
from.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:36.780
Throughout his lifetime, Copernicus was rather
lax when it came to the spelling of his name

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.200
and used, at least, three different variants.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:47.180
Studies in Poland
It is unlikely that Copernicus would have

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:52.170
had the opportunity to pursue a life of science
if not for his maternal uncle, Lucas Watzenrode

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.170
the Younger.

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:57.510
The senior Nicolaus died when his son was
ten years old and his uncle took him and his

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.380
siblings in his care.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:02.150
He ensured a good education for young Copernicus.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:07.000
There are no records to suggest exactly what
that entailed, particularly in his early years,

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.540
but historians have speculated that the young
man most likely followed an educational path

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:12.840
similar to that of his uncle.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:17.310
This would involve studies at St. John’s
School in Toruń, and then the Cathedral School

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:22.020
in Włocławek in order to prepare Copernicus
for entrance to the University of Krakow,

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.020
Watzenrode’s alma mater.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.930
Image suggestion: Lucas Watzenrode
We do know that Copernicus enrolled in the

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:30.980
school in Krakow, today called the Jagiellonian
University, in the winter of 1491.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:35.310
Surprisingly, he first studied arts, but also
decided to learn mathematics in order to take

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:39.690
advantage of the fact that the school had
some of the best mathematicians in Europe.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:44.180
This was also the time when he started going
by the Latin version of his name instead of

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:45.620
his Polish birth name.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:50.660
One of his educators was Albert Brudzewski,
a math teacher who also liked to give lectures

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.450
on astronomy and Aristotelian philosophy.

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:58.450
He was likely among the first to spur young
Copernicus’s interest in studying the stars.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:02.610
The Polish prodigy quickly proved to have
a knack for mathematics and astronomy and

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:07.410
it was during his time at the University of
Krakow that he wrote his first observations

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:08.410
on the subjects.

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:13.140
Nicolaus left the university in 1495 without
a degree and returned home to his uncle.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:17.889
A few years earlier, Lucas Watzenrode the
Younger became the Prince-Bishop of Warmia,

00:04:17.889 --> 00:04:20.909
a special region that was under direct authority
of the pope.

00:04:20.909 --> 00:04:25.039
Unsurprisingly, he wanted to secure a canonical
position for his nephew.

00:04:25.039 --> 00:04:29.189
However, for unclear reasons, Copernicus’s
assignment was delayed so, instead, his uncle

00:04:29.189 --> 00:04:32.860
sent him to continue his studies in Italy
at the Bologna University.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:35.089
Continued Education in Italy
Technically, Copernicus went to Italy to study

00:04:35.089 --> 00:04:38.940
canon law so he could advance his position
in the church, something that would have been

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.770
almost guaranteed given his uncle’s high
standing.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:46.339
However, in the years spent in Bologna, Nicolaus
became more interested in continuing his studies

00:04:46.339 --> 00:04:47.979
of astronomy and the humanities.

00:04:47.979 --> 00:04:53.270
There, he met Domenico Maria Novara, a famed
astronomer of his time who became Copernicus’s

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:56.449
teacher and even took him on as an assistant.

00:04:56.449 --> 00:05:00.460
Copernicus traveled for a bit to Rome where
he spent the jubilee year 1500.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:04.349
By this point, he had become proficient enough
in astronomy that he started giving lectures.

00:05:04.349 --> 00:05:08.439
The next year, the young scientist returned
to Warmia, but the stay was short-lived.

00:05:08.439 --> 00:05:12.659
He convinced the Church that he should also
study medicine, so he was given another two-year

00:05:12.659 --> 00:05:14.080
leave to go back to Italy.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.910
This time, he enrolled at the University of
Padua from 1501 to 1503.

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:22.629
Like before, Copernicus was not one to limit
his knowledge to the intended curriculum and

00:05:22.629 --> 00:05:27.419
pursued multiple interests which, of course,
included astronomy, but also ancient Greek

00:05:27.419 --> 00:05:32.009
which would give him access to many of the
seminal astronomical works from ancient times.

00:05:32.009 --> 00:05:34.710
Back to Warmia
Despite all of his extracurricular activities,

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:39.139
the second time around in Italy Nicolaus managed
to graduate as a Doctor of Law.

00:05:39.139 --> 00:05:42.759
With his studies complete, the 30-year-old
Copernicus returned to Warmia where he would

00:05:42.759 --> 00:05:44.949
spend most of the next 40 years.

00:05:44.949 --> 00:05:47.159
He became his uncle’s right-hand man.

00:05:47.159 --> 00:05:51.249
Officially, he was the Prince-Bishop’s assistant
and physician, although he was involved with

00:05:51.249 --> 00:05:56.389
most of the duties of his uncle, be they religious,
economical, political, or administrative.

00:05:56.389 --> 00:06:00.180
It is unclear if he was ever actually ordained
as a priest, but it does not seem like he

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:02.810
ever took orders in the Catholic Church.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:06.629
With all of his responsibilities, Copernicus
was a busy man and was on the road a lot as

00:06:06.629 --> 00:06:08.279
part of his uncle’s retinue.

00:06:08.279 --> 00:06:12.449
Even so, in the eight years or so that he
served Lucas Watzenrode until his death, he

00:06:12.449 --> 00:06:18.590
still managed to write a small precursor to
his later work that would revolutionize astronomy.

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:26.120
The Almagest
We should probably take a minute to look at

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:29.599
humanity’s understanding of the Universe
prior to Copernicus to truly get a sense of

00:06:29.599 --> 00:06:32.839
how influential he was in the grand scheme
of things.

00:06:32.839 --> 00:06:37.119
Before he published his seminal work, the
astronomical world believed in a geocentric

00:06:37.119 --> 00:06:38.119
model.

00:06:38.119 --> 00:06:42.050
This meant that the Earth was at the center
of the Universe and everything else - moons,

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:45.680
planets, stars, and our Sun - they all orbited
us.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:49.069
Image suggestion: Claudius Ptolemy
This belief appeared in many ancient cultures,

00:06:49.069 --> 00:06:50.069
including the Greeks.

00:06:50.069 --> 00:06:56.179
They all presented, more or less, the basic
tenets of geocentrism, but there was no widely-accepted

00:06:56.179 --> 00:06:57.179
system.

00:06:57.179 --> 00:07:03.279
That is until an astronomer from Alexandria
named Claudius Ptolemy wrote the Almagest

00:07:03.279 --> 00:07:04.780
sometime around the mid second century AD.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:09.599
A quick note here: even if he is sometimes
referred to simply as Ptolemy and he was from

00:07:09.599 --> 00:07:13.819
Alexandria, he should not be confused with
any of the members of the Ptolemaic Dynasty

00:07:13.819 --> 00:07:18.159
that ruled over Egypt for almost 300 years
and also included Cleopatra.

00:07:18.159 --> 00:07:23.300
The astronomer wrote his seminal work which
he called Mathematike Syntaxis or The Mathematical

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:24.300
Compilation.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:28.179
Despite its name, it was mainly about the
motions of the stars and planets and, as it

00:07:28.179 --> 00:07:31.629
grew in influence, it became known as The
Greatest Compilation.

00:07:31.629 --> 00:07:36.149
The name was then shortened to The Greatest
and, later, when it made its way into other

00:07:36.149 --> 00:07:41.809
cultures, it was more commonly referred to
by its Latin transliteration of Almagest.

00:07:41.809 --> 00:07:45.619
Although the Almagest consisted of 13 books,
it had five main points.

00:07:45.619 --> 00:07:47.240
The celestial realm was spherical.

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:51.270
Our Earth was a sphere, at the center of this
realm, and did not move.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:56.679
And, in relation to the distance to all the
fixed stars, the Earth had no appreciable

00:07:56.679 --> 00:08:00.599
size and must have been treated as a mathematical
point.

00:08:00.599 --> 00:08:05.429
Ptolemy’s ideas were popular enough that,
eventually, the Ptolemaic model became synonymous

00:08:05.429 --> 00:08:06.839
with geocentrism.

00:08:06.839 --> 00:08:12.330
It was the accepted truth for 1,300 years,
but it still had its detractors.

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:16.960
Even before Copernicus, there were other astronomers,
particularly in the medieval Arab world, who

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:18.809
saw flaws in Ptolemy’s system.

00:08:18.809 --> 00:09:40.090
However, none of them went so far as to propose
an alternative that completely negated it.

00:09:40.090 --> 00:09:51.920
The Commentariolus
Because the Almagest had been around for well

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:56.610
over a thousand years and traveled from culture
to culture, it has been translated many times

00:09:56.610 --> 00:09:58.260
over from one language to another.

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:02.779
Written originally in Greek, it was converted
into Arabic by Muslim scholars and, later,

00:10:02.779 --> 00:10:06.710
medieval Europeans used that version and translated
it into Latin.

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:10.990
Many astronomers prior to Copernicus thought
that it might contain inaccuracies not necessarily

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:15.000
due to Ptolemy’s faulty ideas, but due to
poor translations.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:18.050
One of them was a German scientist called
Regiomontanus.

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:22.520
He obtained the original Greek work and concluded
that there were errors, in fact, which belonged

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:26.030
to Ptolemy rather than the translators who
came after him.

00:10:26.030 --> 00:10:31.280
He published the Summary of Ptolemy’s Almagest
several years before Copernicus was born.

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:33.570
The Polish astronomer read Regiomontanus’s
book.

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:38.310
He had also been spurred on by his teacher
in Bologna, Domenico Maria Novara, who was

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:41.200
another skeptic who doubted Ptolemy’s infallibility.

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:46.300
Nicolaus was determined to study the ancient
Greek astronomer himself and draw his own

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:47.300
conclusions.

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:52.470
Sometime before 1514, Copernicus wrote a treatise
called the Commentariolus (Little Commentary).

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:57.360
Consisting of eight chapters, it made arguments
for seven postulates that laid down the groundwork

00:10:57.360 --> 00:10:59.060
for his heliocentric model.

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:02.400
Crucially, they said that the Earth is not
the center of the Universe and that there

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:06.550
is no fixed point around which all other celestial
bodies orbit.

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:10.720
The paper got some things right, such as the
fact that the Moon is the only sphere that

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:15.050
orbits the Earth and that the distance from
the Earth to the Sun is insignificant compared

00:11:15.050 --> 00:11:17.380
to the distance to all the other stars.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:21.660
Copernicus also placed the known planets in
the right order from the Sun and calculated

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:24.520
their orbital periods with pretty impressive
accuracy.

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:28.530
It also got some things wrong, such as placing
the Sun near the center of the Universe and

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:31.150
concluding that the stars are immovable.

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:37.190
Even so, these radical ideas were very far
removed from the accepted model of Ptolemy.

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:41.800
Copernicus saw his Commentariolus as just
an outline for a much grander work.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:45.490
Therefore, he did not make many copies of
his treatise and only shared it with a few

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:46.490
other people.

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:48.200
It was never published during his lifetime.

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:51.370
Among those who received a copy of the paper,
the Commentariolus garnered a good reception.

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:55.260
It was of particular interest to Nikolaus
von Schönberg, the Archbishop of Capua and

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:57.810
later a Cardinal of Pope Paul III.

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:02.350
In 1536, he wrote a letter to Copernicus requesting
a copy of his treatise.

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:06.320
He praised the astronomer’s “so fine a
talent” and expressed great interest in

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:08.220
his revolutionary ideas.

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:12.560
Copernicus later included this letter in his
magnum opus, De revolutionibus orbium coelestium.

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.980
In the Meantime
The Polish scientist was not one to rush things.

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:20.770
After he wrote the Commentariolus, he would
spend another two decades working on his main

00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:21.770
treatise.

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:25.110
It would be another decade after that that
it would finally get printed, in the same

00:12:25.110 --> 00:12:26.860
year that Copernicus died.

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:31.840
He was mainly concerned with his astronomical
observations, but Copernicus also had plenty

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:35.460
of administrative duties to attend to which
ate up a lot of his time.

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:40.270
His uncle, the Prince-Bishop of Warmia, died
in 1512 and Nicolaus participated in the election

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:41.490
of his successor.

00:12:41.490 --> 00:12:45.770
There were also other issues that required
his attention such as renewed war with the

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:51.740
Teutonic Order and internal strife among the
leaders of Warmia over how loyal and servient

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:53.400
they should be towards the Polish Crown.

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:54.400
Image suggestion: Copernicus tower
https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copernicus_tower.jpg

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:58.740
Around this time, Copernicus also relocated
permanently to the city of Frombork where

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:00.840
he lived and worked for the remainder of his
life.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:05.000
He not only had a house, but was also allowed
to purchase the northwestern tower within

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:07.120
the stronghold walls to use as his observatory.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:16.290
Copernicus the Economist
Often overlooked is Copernicus’s contribution

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:17.290
to economics.

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:21.350
As mentioned before, he wore many hats as
his uncle’s right-hand man.

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:25.970
When Lucas Watzenrode passed away, his nephew
proved himself adept enough that he continued

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:29.250
to be a major influence in the administration
of Warmia.

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:34.780
In 1526, he was asked by King Sigismund I
of Poland to write a paper on monetary reform

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:37.380
which was a hot button topic at the time.

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:42.070
Copernicus wrote Monetae cudendae ratio (“On
the Minting of Coin”).

00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:46.520
The gist of it is exemplified by the principle
that “bad money drives out good”.

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:51.040
In this case, “good” money refers to coins
whose face value is roughly the same as their

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:54.550
commodity value granted by the value of the
metals they are made from.

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:59.140
“Bad” money refers to coins that not only
have a significant disparity between the two

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:02.920
values, but are also put in circulation alongside
“good” money.

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:07.560
A bit unfairly, the postulate is known today
as Gresham’s Law, named in the 19th century

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:10.990
after 16th century English financier Sir Thomas
Gresham.

00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:17.650
However, Copernicus published his work on
the subject decades before Gresham.

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:23.279
Delays
By the early 1530s, Copernicus’s seminal

00:14:23.279 --> 00:14:27.779
work De revolutionibus orbium coelestium was
finished, more or less.

00:14:27.779 --> 00:14:31.750
He kept on making changes to it until his
death, but it could have been ready for printing,

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:32.750
if he desired it.

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:37.490
However, the Polish astronomer kept on delaying
the publishing of his book and, even today,

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:40.400
scholars argue over the true reason for this
behavior.

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:43.940
Some feel like it had to do with the Church
and that Copernicus feared the consequences

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:47.020
that might occur if his work was deemed heretical.

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:49.900
After all, things did not work out so well
for one of the strongest advocates of Copernicanism,

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:50.900
Galileo.

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:53.850
Almost a hundred years later, his ideas were
considered heresy by the Roman Inquisition

00:14:53.850 --> 00:14:58.820
because heliocentrism contradicted the Holy
Scripture and, as a result, Galileo was forced

00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:03.040
to recant his views and he spent the last
decade of his life under house arrest.

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:07.050
Others believe that Copernicus was reticent
to publish his findings because he feared

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:10.040
any kind of criticism or outright rejection
of his ideas.

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:12.820
The Book
Ever since Copernicus wrote the Commentariolus,

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:17.070
he was careful to share his work only with
friends and other trusted astronomers.

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:21.529
He did the same thing with De revolutionibus
orbium coelestium prior to printing, but word

00:15:21.529 --> 00:15:23.790
of mouth spread and others heard of it.

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:25.480
Bishops knew about it.

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:26.690
So did the Pope.

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:30.570
Initial reactions seemed to be either of approval
or indifference.

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:35.310
There were plenty of people, astronomers included,
who disagreed with his ideas, but no one was

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:39.800
beating down the door of his house to burn
Copernicus at the stake just yet.

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:44.130
A contributing factor had to be the fact that
his book was not exactly “reader friendly”.

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:48.339
Copernicus believed that “mathematics is
written for mathematicians” so laymen were

00:15:48.339 --> 00:15:51.190
unlikely to get much out of it, even if they
read it.

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:55.670
It was up to other astronomers who came afterwards
to champion his cause and bring Copernican

00:15:55.670 --> 00:16:00.270
beliefs to the wider masses (and suffer the
consequences for it).

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:03.850
The basic idea was the one initially outlined
in Commentariolus.

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:09.170
According to heliocentrism, the Earth was
no longer special - it was just another planet

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:13.570
that orbited the Sun.

00:16:13.570 --> 00:16:18.339
Rheticus
One of the aforementioned astronomers who

00:16:18.339 --> 00:16:24.181
became followers of Copernicus was his pupil,
Georg Joachim de Porris, better known as Rheticus.

00:16:24.181 --> 00:16:26.680
Image: Rheticus
When he was young, Rheticus obtained the patronage

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:31.090
of Philipp Melanchthon, an influential Protestant
reformer and Martin Luther’s “right-hand

00:16:31.090 --> 00:16:32.090
man”.

00:16:32.090 --> 00:16:34.750
He arranged for the young scientist to go
study with some of the greatest astronomers

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:38.250
of the day, but it was Copernicus who garnered
his true interest.

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:42.830
In fact, he might have played the most important
part in making sure that De revolutionibus

00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:46.600
orbium coelestium saw the light of day, after
Copernicus, of course.

00:16:46.600 --> 00:16:50.190
He kept on pestering and encouraging his teacher
to publish his work.

00:16:50.190 --> 00:16:55.100
In 1540, with the permission of Copernicus,
Rheticus wrote and published Narratio Prima

00:16:55.100 --> 00:16:56.100
(“The First Report”).

00:16:56.100 --> 00:17:00.710
It was an abstract of his teacher’s heliocentric
theory and served well as an introduction

00:17:00.710 --> 00:17:04.140
to Copernicus’s book which would be released
a few years later.

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:09.569
So, yes, the first ever published work on
the Copernican model did not even belong to

00:17:09.569 --> 00:17:10.569
Copernicus.

00:17:10.569 --> 00:17:14.409
Even so, nobody raised their pitchforks in
anger when this work came out, and this proved

00:17:14.409 --> 00:17:19.660
to be instrumental in finally convincing the
Polish astronomer to put his own book into

00:17:19.660 --> 00:17:20.660
print.

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:22.980
Ad Lectorem
In 1543, Copernicus finally consented to having

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:24.360
his magnum opus published.

00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:29.390
He sent it via Rheticus to German printer
Johannes Petreius and the project was overseen

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:31.809
by theologian Andreas Osiander.

00:17:31.809 --> 00:17:36.299
He had previously conversed with Copernicus
and showed an interest in his hypotheses,

00:17:36.299 --> 00:17:39.749
but it is not clear if he undertook this task
on his own or at someone else’s behest.

00:17:39.749 --> 00:17:42.700
Image: Andreas Osiander
The book was divided into six sections, mimicking

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:45.400
closely the layout of Ptolemy’s Almagest.

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:50.210
It was dedicated to Pope Paul III and contained
a preface which argued that mathematics was

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:55.179
the basis used for these new ideas and not
physics so mathematics should be used to understand

00:17:55.179 --> 00:17:56.179
them.

00:17:56.179 --> 00:17:59.410
These were all efforts by Copernicus to make
his work less controversial in the eyes of

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:00.670
the Catholic Church.

00:18:00.670 --> 00:18:03.539
When De revolutionibus orbium coelestium finally
came out, Copernicus couldn’t help but notice

00:18:03.539 --> 00:18:08.529
that the preface also contained a letter that
he did not write or even know about.

00:18:08.529 --> 00:18:10.240
It was titled Ad lectorem de hypothesibus
huius operis (“To the reader concerning

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:15.300
the hypotheses of this work”) and had actually
been written by Osiander and added to the

00:18:15.300 --> 00:18:17.440
book without Copernicus’s consent.

00:18:17.440 --> 00:18:22.710
The addition advised that the ideas put forward
in the book are not necessarily true, or even

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:28.179
probable, for that matter, but they are useful
for making observations and calculating distances.

00:18:28.179 --> 00:18:32.559
It anticipated that scholars would be “deeply
offended” by the notion that the Earth moves

00:18:32.559 --> 00:18:36.730
while the Sun is at rest in the center of
the Universe and came to the defence of the

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:39.360
author who “has done nothing blameworthy”.

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:43.169
The thing about the letter was that Osiander
wrote it anonymously and did not sign anything

00:18:43.169 --> 00:18:44.169
at the end.

00:18:44.169 --> 00:18:48.740
To the reader, it looked like Copernicus himself
was the author of Ad Lectorem who, seemingly,

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:51.510
didn’t believe his own theories.

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:58.700
Reactions and Receptions
Copernicus wasn’t around for long to see

00:18:58.700 --> 00:18:59.879
the impact of his work.

00:18:59.879 --> 00:19:02.370
He died of a stroke soon after De revolutionibus
orbium coelestium was published in his home

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:03.370
in Frombork.

00:19:03.370 --> 00:19:07.340
He was 70 years old and a legend says that
he passed away right after being presented

00:19:07.340 --> 00:19:12.080
with the final printed pages of the book he
worked on for most of his life.

00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:16.929
As far as Ad Lectorem was concerned, Rheticus
and supporters of Copernicus made sure that

00:19:16.929 --> 00:19:20.320
others knew that the Polish scientist was
not the one who wrote it.

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:23.169
Osiander’s reason for writing it still remains
a mystery, though.

00:19:23.169 --> 00:19:27.120
Some argue that he himself did not believe
in Copernicus’s theories and was offended

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:32.149
by their heretical implications and wanted
to dismiss the book as a legitimate work of

00:19:32.149 --> 00:19:33.149
science.

00:19:33.149 --> 00:19:36.550
Others believe the exact opposite - Osiander
tried to minimize the negative reactions to

00:19:36.550 --> 00:19:38.379
the book as much as possible.

00:19:38.379 --> 00:19:41.619
Your guess is as good as anyone’s regarding
his true intentions.

00:19:41.619 --> 00:19:46.379
De revolutionibus orbium coelestium won’t
be cracking any best-seller lists anytime

00:19:46.379 --> 00:19:47.379
soon.

00:19:47.379 --> 00:19:51.429
It sold terrible because, as we mentioned
before, it was very technical and not meant

00:19:51.429 --> 00:19:55.330
for anyone who didn’t have a solid background
in mathematics or astronomy.

00:19:55.330 --> 00:19:59.350
The few copies that were sold, though, reached
the right people and, in the decades that

00:19:59.350 --> 00:20:01.970
followed, many scientists were converted to
Copernicanism.

00:20:01.970 --> 00:20:07.070
Computations using this new model were, unsurprisingly,
more accurate, and proved very useful for

00:20:07.070 --> 00:20:08.450
new astronomical tables.

00:20:08.450 --> 00:20:11.190
Also unsurprising was the religious reaction.

00:20:11.190 --> 00:20:13.909
Protestants were the ones most fired up against
Copernicus.

00:20:13.909 --> 00:20:18.360
Martin Luther objected that his work went
against Holy Scripture that said that “Joshua

00:20:18.360 --> 00:20:21.190
bid the sun to stand still and not the earth”.

00:20:21.190 --> 00:20:26.529
He believed Copernicus was a fool who wanted
“to turn the whole art of astronomy upside-down”.

00:20:26.529 --> 00:20:30.039
The Catholic Church had a similar, but much
slower reaction, and put De revolutionibus

00:20:30.039 --> 00:20:34.519
orbium coelestium on the Index of Forbidden
Books in the early 17th century, almost 70

00:20:34.519 --> 00:20:36.789
years after it originally came out.

00:20:36.789 --> 00:20:39.980
Try as they might, though, the idea of heliocentrism
was now in the open.

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:44.179
This probably meant little to the common man,
but more and more scientists looked at it

00:20:44.179 --> 00:20:46.110
and realized that it made sense.

00:20:46.110 --> 00:20:50.590
It was a bell that could not be unrung and
our understanding of the Universe grew and

00:20:50.590 --> 00:21:05.789
evolved from that premise to what

00:21:05.789 --> 00:21:11.600
we have today.

